    <item>
      <title>Little Bird: this beautiful, moving Canadian drama will fill you with visceral anger</title>
      <link>https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/2026/aug/15/little-bird-canadian-drama-first-nations-children-sixties-scoop-adoptions</link>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;There is dignity and tenderness to this drama based on the real story of the Sixties Scoop – the scandal of First Nations children who were forcibly taken from their parents and adopted by white families&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Here’s one you’ll like: a man comes home to find his wife handcuffed to the wall and his young children missing. They have been taken by child protection services due to the lack of a fridge in the house, which is treated as a sign of neglect. When he confronts law enforcement officials outside, they beat him to death. Decades later, one of the children, now adopted into a Jewish home in Montreal, goes looking for the family she lost, risking the collapse of her new life to do so. I never said it was a joke. Although it turns out there was a fridge, behind the house.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Watching Little Bird (18 August, 9pm, Sky Atlantic), I was reminded that TV is capable of more than mere entertainment, something we lose sight of when we think of it as a purely profit-or loss-making enterprise. I felt the visceral anger and sadness of this story of a First Nations woman caught between cultures, robbed by forces beyond her control and stonewalled by the authorities. Set in Canada, the attitude, if not the events, feel shamefully familiar.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/2026/aug/15/little-bird-canadian-drama-first-nations-children-sixties-scoop-adoptions"&gt;Continue reading...&lt;/a&gt;</description>
